	Dave, I'd like to ask you to do a favor for me.  But first,
I want to give the rest of rgrn some background information:


	Games written for the PC and games written for consoles (like
the Genesis) don't quite work the same.  The consoles have relatively
poor storage capabilities.  This strongly affects how game-saving
works.  (Example:  Doom on the Playstation never saves games.  In-
stead, after finishing a level, you're given a "password", which is
really your current level, health, and ammo, encoded into ASCII.)

	I read PC Gamer magazine.  Games ported over from consoles are
reviewed fairly often.  The reviewers almost always dislike these
console ports.  They consistently complain about the same two things:

   o  "This game only saves at particular spots.  Bah!"  

   o  "This game is too easy.  Bah!"  (Or, once in a while, too hard.)
      (Doom on the Playstation is easier in maybe half a dozen 
      different ways.  BUT.  Not being able to save your game in mid-
      level makes it sharply harder.)

	Rephrasing the above:  PC games have evolved a standard for
how game-saving works.  The console ports do not follow this stand-
ard, even after the game-saving behaviour has been worked over a bit 
for the PC.  And, after the game-saving behaviour has been worked
over, the games are too easy.

	A variation of this applies to Nethack.

	Nethack was designed long before there was a standard for
how game-saving works.  Since Nethack doesn't resemble modern 
shooters worth beans, Nethack's design for how game-saving works is 
fairly far from the modern standard.  This is no big deal until
someone familiar with the modern standard starts playing Nethack.  
At this point, we get a sequence something like:

1.    "This game doesn't let you save properly.  Bah!"

2.    "Wait:  I can fake up proper game-saving behaviour with just a
       bit of work."  (Note that the player "knows" that there is a 
       problem, and "knows" that his solution is perfectly reasonable
       and is perfectly justified.)

3.     People on rgrn scream, beat their heads against the wall, and
       set themselves on fire in protest.  Then they get upset.

4.     The new player leaves rgrn.

5.     The new player finds the game too easy and either gives up on
       Nethack or stops backing up save files.


	Let's consider a different sequence of events:

1.    "This game doesn't let you save properly.  Bah!"

2.    "Wait:  I just have to use Explore mode."

3.     People on rgrn yawn, scratch their heads, and take a nap out
       of sheer boredom.  They then tell the new player that Explore
       mode is too easy, and to give it up if he wants to improve.

4.     The new player continues to participate in rgrn.

5.     "I've ascended once in Explore mode!  Now I'll try playing
        in normal mode, just like you guys do."


	Almost all new players are going to be thoroughly familiar
with the PC's standard for game-saving.  When they find out that
Nethack is savagely difficult, some of them will "fix" things by
backing up their saved games.  They will feel completely justified
in this.  Given their experience with other games, Nethack's game-
saving behaviour is clearly broken, right? 

